      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To determine the number of students who did not receive an A in either math or history, we can use the principle of inclusion and exclusion. Here's the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. **Determine the number of students who received an A in history:**\n   Let \\( A_{\\text{hist}} \\) be the set of students who received an A in history. We know:\n   \\[\n   |A_{\\text{hist}}| = 7\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the number of students who received an A in math:**\n   Let \\( A_{\\text{math}} \\) be the set of students who received an A in math. We know:\n   \\[\n   |A_{\\text{math}}| = 13\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the number of students who received an A in both courses:**\n   Let \\( A_{\\text{both}} \\) be the set of students who received an A in both courses. We know:\n   \\[\n   |A_{\\text{both}}| = 4\n   \\]\n\n4. **Apply the principle of inclusion and exclusion to find the total number of students who received an A in either course:**\n   The principle of inclusion and exclusion for two sets states:\n   \\[\n   |A_{\\text{hist}} \\cup A_{\\text{math}}| = |A_{\\text{hist}}| + |A_{\\text{math}}| - |A_{\\text{both}}|\n   \\]\n   Substituting the known values:\n   \\[\n   |A_{\\text{hist}} \\cup A_{\\text{math}}| = 7 + 13 - 4 = 16\n   \\]\n   So, 16 students received an A in either math or history or both.\n\n5. **Calculate the number of students who did not receive an A in any of these two courses:**\n   Since there are 30 students in total, the number of students who did not receive an A in either course is:\n   \\[\n   30 - |A_{\\text{hist}} \\cup A_{\\text{math}}| = 30 - 16 = 14\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of students who did not receive an A in any of these two courses is \\(\\boxed{14}\\).",
